window.CD_COURSE={"modules":[[1,"The compiler & lexical analysis","Turn characters into meaningful tokens","Phases, regex, direct DFA and Lex"],[2,"Syntax analysis","Make sense of token sequences","FIRST/FOLLOW, LL(1), shift-reduce, SLR, CLR, LALR"],[3,"Semantic analysis","Give grammar a meaning","SDD, SDT, attributes, dependency graphs"],[4,"Intermediate code","Translate meaning into simple instructions","TAC, arrays, declarations, Boolean flow, backpatching"],[5,"Code optimization","Remove wasted work without changing meaning","Basic blocks, DAG, dataflow, loops, JIT and verification"],[6,"Code generation","Turn IR into efficient machine instructions","Costs, next use, registers, runtime storage"],[7,"Parallelism & SSA","Make independent work run together","Dependence, scheduling, software pipelining, SSA"]],"lessons":[[1,"The six phases of a compiler","Phases of a Compiler pp. 2–19","A compiler is a translator that checks and transforms a program, not just a word replacer. Each phase changes the representation of the same program.",["Lexical analysis: characters → tokens; syntax analysis: tokens → parse tree/AST.","Semantic analysis: check types, declarations and scope; insert permitted coercions.","Intermediate code generation: produce machine-independent IR, e.g. three-address code.","Optimization: improve IR without changing observable behavior; code generation: choose instructions and registers.","Symbol table management and error reporting support multiple phases. Front end is generally source-oriented; back end target-oriented."],"position = initial + rate * 60\nLEX: id = id + id * num\nAST: =(position, +(initial, *(rate,60)))\nSEM: convert 60 to float if rate is float and coercion permitted\nIR: t1 = inttofloat(60); t2 = rate * t1; position = initial + t2\nOPT: t2 = rate * 60.0; position = initial + t2\nTARGET (illustrative): LDF R1,rate; MULF R1,#60.0; ADDF R1,initial; STF position,R1","Syntax errors concern structure; semantic errors concern meaning. A legal-looking expression may still have incompatible types.","Which phase inserts a permitted int-to-float conversion?","Semantic analysis, guided by the language's type rules.","pipeline"],[1,"Tokens, patterns, lexemes & attributes","Lexical Analysis: Design Issues pp. 1–9","A token is a category, a lexeme is the actual text, and a pattern describes the text that belongs to a token category.",["Lexical analyzer scans the stream, groups characters into lexemes and returns tokens with optional attributes.","Patterns can be regular expressions; identifiers may carry pointers to symbol table entries; numeric literals carry values.","Whitespace/comments are usually skipped, while line/column information helps error reporting.","Longest matching lexeme wins in conventional Lex; equal lengths use rule order. Keywords must be distinguished from identifiers.","Lexical errors may use panic-mode recovery (skip characters until a usable token) or local repair."],"int count = 42;\nlexeme   token       attribute\nint      KEYWORD     int\ncount    ID          symbol entry for count\n=        ASSIGN      —\n42       NUMBER      42\n;        SEMICOLON   —","A token is not necessarily a single character. == should be one operator token, not two assignments.","For count42, what is the token vs lexeme?","Token ID; lexeme count42; attribute may reference the symbol table.","lexer"],[1,"Regular expressions & regular definitions","Specification of Tokens pp. 2–13","Regex describes strings, not grammar productions. It is the scanner's rulebook.",["Alphabet Σ is a finite set of symbols; a string is a finite sequence; ε is the empty string, ∅ the empty language.","Union r|s means either; concatenation rs means r followed by s; Kleene star r* means zero or more repetitions.","Precedence: closure before concatenation before union. Parentheses override it.","Regular definitions name patterns: digit=[0-9], letter=[A-Za-z], id=letter(letter|digit)*.","Shorthands r+ = rr*, r? = ε|r. Regular languages cannot enforce arbitrary nested balanced parentheses."],"digit = [0-9]\nletter = [A-Za-z]\nid = letter(letter|digit)*\nnumber = digit+(.digit+)?  (dot here is a literal decimal point)\n(a|b)*abb: any a/b prefix ending in abb","In a CFG such as E→E+T, + is a literal terminal, not a regex closure. In regex, escape a literal + if the notation requires it.","Does (a|b)*abb accept aabb? Does it accept abba?","Yes; no. The suffix must be exactly abb.","regex"],[1,"NFA, DFA and direct regex → DFA","Recognition of Tokens pp. 5–17","A DFA is a machine with one next state per state/symbol. An NFA may have multiple choices or ε moves. The direct method skips constructing an NFA.",["Augment regex with a unique end marker # and build its syntax tree. Number every non-ε leaf from left to right.","nullable tells whether subtree accepts ε; firstpos and lastpos tell possible first/last numbered symbols.","For union: nullable OR, first/last unions. For concatenation XY: nullable AND, first(X) plus first(Y) if X nullable; last(Y) plus last(X) if Y nullable. Star is nullable and inherits first/last.","For concatenation XY, add firstpos(Y) to followpos(i) for each i in lastpos(X). For star X*, add firstpos(X) to followpos(i) for each i in lastpos(X).","Initial DFA state is firstpos(root); for a symbol a, union followpos of all a-labelled positions in current set. Any state containing # is accepting. Repeat until no new sets."],"(a|b)*abb#  positions a1,b2,a3,b4,b5,#6\nfollow(1)={1,2,3}; follow(2)={1,2,3}; follow(3)={4}; follow(4)={5}; follow(5)={6}; follow(6)=∅\nA={1,2,3}: a→B, b→A\nB={1,2,3,4}: a→B, b→C\nC={1,2,3,5}: a→B, b→D\nD={1,2,3,6}: a→B, b→A (accept)\nTrace aabb: A→B→B→C→D","Positions identify occurrences, not distinct letters. The first a and suffix a have different positions. # is not a real input character.","Why is D accepting?","It contains position 6, the end marker #.","dfa"],[1,"LEX — generate a scanner","Lex: A Lexical Analyzer Generator pp. 2–9","Rather than hand-writing a giant character loop, specify patterns and actions. Lex generates a scanner from them.",["A Lex file contains definitions, rules, and auxiliary C routines separated by %% markers.","A rule consists of a regular expression and an action. yytext holds the matched lexeme; yyleng holds its length; yylex() drives scanning.","Lex source → lex/flex → lex.yy.c → C compiler → executable scanner.","Example rules return token codes, store attributes or ignore whitespace. A final catch-all handles unknown characters.","Longest match first; ties resolve to the earlier rule. A keyword pattern should precede an equally long identifier pattern."],"%{\\n/* token declarations */\\n%}\ndigit [0-9]\n%%\n\"if\"      { return IF; }\n[a-zA-Z][a-zA-Z0-9]* { return ID; }\n{digit}+  { return NUM; }\n[ \\t\\n]+ ;\n.         { /* lexical error */ }\n%%\n/* auxiliary functions */","Do not confuse Lex with Yacc: Lex finds tokens, Yacc parses token sequences.","What does yytext contain after matching 123?","The matched text \"123\", while yyleng is 3.","lexer"],[2,"CFGs, derivations, parse trees & ambiguity","Module 2 (2025) pp. 2–14","A grammar is a recipe for building valid sentences. A parser discovers how tokens fit that recipe.",["CFG G=(V,T,P,S): nonterminals, terminals, productions, start symbol. ε is empty; $ marks end of input in parsing.","A derivation rewrites a nonterminal; leftmost expands leftmost, rightmost expands rightmost.","Parse tree shows the production hierarchy; yield is the terminal sequence at leaves.","Ambiguous grammar permits two distinct parse trees for at least one string. E→E+E|E*E|id is ambiguous for id+id*id.","Resolve ordinary arithmetic precedence by using E→E+T|T, T→T*F|F, F→(E)|id. Left recursion supports left associativity in this grammar."],"id+id*id\nAmbiguous interpretations: +(id,*(id,id)) or *(+(id,id),id)\nUnambiguous grammar separates + at E from * at T, so multiplication binds tighter.","Ambiguity is a property of a grammar, not merely the presence of multiple productions. Leftmost and rightmost derivations of the same tree do not prove ambiguity.","Why are + and * literal here?","They are terminals in a CFG; regular expression operators are a different notation.","tree"],[2,"Top-down, recursive descent & error recovery","Module 2 pp. 2–7, 15–24","Top-down predicts which rule will build the input; bottom-up recognizes which completed pieces can be reduced.",["Recursive descent implements a routine per nonterminal; general form may backtrack. Predictive recursive descent chooses using lookahead without backtracking for LL(1).","Nonrecursive predictive parsing uses a stack, input buffer and table M[A,a].","Parser recovery goals: report clearly, recover quickly, avoid excessive cost.","Panic mode discards tokens to a synchronizing token; phrase-level recovery performs local repair; error productions handle known mistakes; global correction seeks minimal changes (costly).","Top-down builds from root to leaves and follows leftmost derivation; bottom-up constructs from leaves and reverses a rightmost derivation."],"parseF(): if lookahead==id: match(id)\n          else if lookahead=='(': match('('); parseE(); match(')')\n          else: report syntax error","A recursive descent parser can be predictive, but not every recursive descent parser is predictive.","Why would direct left recursion break ordinary recursive descent?","It calls the same routine without consuming a token, potentially recursing forever.",""],[2,"Eliminate left recursion & left factor","Module 2 pp. 12–14, 25–27","Remove patterns that stop a top-down parser from making a decision.",["Immediate left recursion A→Aα|β becomes A→βA′ and A′→αA′|ε; use all β alternatives and all α alternatives.","Indirect recursion: order nonterminals, substitute earlier alternatives into later productions, then remove immediate recursion.","Left factoring A→αβ1|αβ2|γ becomes A→αA′|γ; A′→β1|β2. Extract longest shared prefixes iteratively.","These transformations preserve language under standard conditions, not necessarily the original parse tree shape. Preserve intended associativity in semantic actions."],"E→E+T|T becomes E→TE′; E′→+TE′|ε\nT→T*F|F becomes T→FT′; T′→*FT′|ε\nA→ab|ac|d becomes A→aA′|d; A′→b|c","Left factoring does not magically eliminate all ambiguity or make every grammar LL(1).","Transform A→Aa|b.","A→bA′; A′→aA′|ε.","rewrite"],[2,"FIRST & FOLLOW — the foundation","Module 2 pp. 28–40","FIRST tells what can begin a string. FOLLOW tells what can immediately come after a nonterminal.",["FIRST(terminal) is itself; FIRST(ε)={ε}. Scan a RHS left to right, union FIRST without ε until a nonnullable symbol; add ε if all nullable.","FOLLOW(start) contains $. For A→αBβ, add FIRST(β)−{ε} to FOLLOW(B). If β can derive ε (including empty suffix), add FOLLOW(A).","Repeat both computations until no set changes. FOLLOW never contains ε.","Use FIRST of the whole RHS, not just the first symbol when it can disappear."],"E→T E'; E'→+ T E'|ε; T→F T'; T'→* F T'|ε; F→( E )|id\nFIRST(E)=FIRST(T)=FIRST(F)={(,id}\nFIRST(E')={+,ε}; FIRST(T')={*,ε}\nFOLLOW(E)=FOLLOW(E')={),$}\nFOLLOW(T)=FOLLOW(T')={+,),$}\nFOLLOW(F)={*,+,),$}","FOLLOW does not mean what follows a production on the printed page; it is about possible sentential forms.","Why is + in FOLLOW(T)?","In E→T E′, + belongs to FIRST(E′), excluding ε.","grammar"],[2,"LL(1) table & stack trace","Module 2 pp. 30–40","LL(1) means Left-to-right scan, Leftmost derivation, 1 token of lookahead. Fill a grid that tells the parser which production to use.",["For A→α, put it in M[A,a] for all a∈FIRST(α)−{ε}. If α nullable, put it in M[A,b] for all b∈FOLLOW(A).","Multiple distinct productions in a cell = conflict, so grammar not LL(1). Empty entry = syntax error.","Start stack $ S, with top at right. If top terminal equals lookahead, pop and advance. If nonterminal, look up production, pop and push RHS in reverse order (omit ε).","Accept when stack and input both reach $. Show stack, remaining input and action in each exam row."],"Grammar: E→T E'; E'→+ T E'|ε; T→F T'; T'→* F T'|ε; F→( E )|id\nM[E,id]=E→TE′; M[F,id]=F→id\nM[E′,+]=E′→+TE′; M[T′,*]=T′→*FT′\nM[T′,+]=T′→ε; M[E′,$]=E′→ε\nTrace id+id*id$ with the interactive table.","Never push ε onto the parsing stack. $ is an end marker, not a source-language operator.","What happens at top T′ and lookahead +?","Apply T′→ε because + belongs to FOLLOW(T′).","grammar"],[2,"Shift-reduce, handles & operator precedence","Module 2 pp. 41–62","Bottom-up parsing assembles larger grammar structures from the tokens already read.",["Shift pushes the next input token; reduce replaces a handle (RHS of a valid reverse rightmost derivation step) by its LHS; accept or error.","Handle pruning reverses a rightmost derivation. A viable prefix is a stack prefix that does not extend past the right end of a handle.","Shift/reduce conflict: either action possible. Reduce/reduce: two productions compete.","Operator grammar: no ε productions and no adjacent nonterminals (usual operator-precedence setting). FIRSTVT/LASTVT capture possible boundary terminals including those next to a leading/trailing nonterminal.","For A→…ab… or aBb, set a =· b; A→…aB… gives a <· FIRSTVT(B); A→…Bb… gives LASTVT(B) >· b. Set $<·FIRSTVT(S), LASTVT(S)>·$; multiple relations cause conflict.","Precedence functions f,g encode relations as inequalities when consistent; blank/error entries still need checking."],"E→E+T|T; T→T*F|F; F→id\nTrace id+id*id: shift id; reduce F,T,E; shift +; shift id; reduce F,T; shift *; shift id; reduce F; reduce T→T*F; reduce E→E+T; accept. For a full state-based trace use the LR workshop.","A handle is not simply any matching substring. With E→E+E|E*E|id, precedence conflicts must be resolved explicitly.","What relation should + have to * when multiplication has higher precedence?","+ <· * (shift the higher-precedence operator).",""],[2,"LR(0) items, closure and GOTO","Module 2 pp. 63–75","An LR item is a production with a progress marker dot. The dot tells how much of a rule has already been recognized.",["Augment grammar S′→S. Start closure({S′→·S}).","CLOSURE: whenever A→α·Bβ is present and B is a nonterminal, add B→·γ for every B production; repeat.","GOTO(I,X): move the dot over X in every matching item, then take closure of the resulting set.","Canonical collection: start at I0, calculate GOTO for each symbol, add distinct nonempty sets until stable.","A completed item A→α· signals a possible reduction; S′→S· signals acceptance on $. LR stack stores states and symbols."],"S′→S; S→CC; C→cC|d\nI0: S′→·S, S→·CC, C→·cC, C→·d\nGOTO(I0,c): C→c·C, C→·cC, C→·d\nGOTO(I0,d): C→d·\nGOTO(I0,S): S′→S·","A state is a SET of items, not a single item. Closure expands only a nonterminal immediately after the dot.","What happens if the symbol after the dot is terminal c?","Closure adds nothing for c; GOTO on c shifts the dot.","lr"],[2,"SLR parsing tables & input validation","Module 2 pp. 67–77","SLR uses LR(0) item states but only allows a completed production to reduce on FOLLOW of its left side.",["Build augmented grammar and LR(0) canonical collection. Number productions and states.","If GOTO(Ii,a)=Ij for terminal a, ACTION[i,a]=sj. For nonterminal A, GOTO table[i,A]=j.","For completed A→α·, A≠S′, enter reduce r on every a∈FOLLOW(A). For S′→S·, ACTION[$]=accept.","Any conflicting action means not SLR(1). To parse, shift states; on reduction A→β pop |β| symbols and states (ε pops none), then push A and GOTO from exposed state.","Use the actual state numbering of your automaton consistently; another valid numbering yields another equivalent table."],"S→CC; C→cC|d\nFOLLOW(C)={c,d,$}; FOLLOW(S)={$}\nIn state {C→d·}, reduce C→d on c,d,$.\nIn accepting state {S′→S·}, accept only on $.","Do not reduce on every terminal as in LR(0). SLR reductions use FOLLOW(A).","If a completed item is C→d· and lookahead c∈FOLLOW(C), what entry?","Reduce using the production C→d.","lr"],[2,"CLR / canonical LR(1)","Module 2 pp. 78–86","SLR uses a broad global FOLLOW set. CLR carries a local lookahead inside each item, so reductions are more precise.",["Item [A→α·β,a] includes a one-symbol lookahead a.","Closure: if [A→α·Bβ,a], add [B→·γ,b] for every b∈FIRST(βa). Repeat to a fixed point.","GOTO advances dot and preserves each item's lookahead, then takes closure.","Canonical states are equal only if their full LR(1) item sets match, including lookaheads. Shift/GOTO as usual.","For completed [A→α·,a], reduce only on a; accept [S′→S·,$]. CLR often has more states than SLR/LALR."],"S→CC; C→cC|d\nI0 starts [S′→·S,$], [S→·CC,$]\nFor first C, FIRST(C$)={c,d}, so [C→·cC,c/d] and [C→·d,c/d].\nAfter recognizing first C, the next C gets lookahead $.","Lookahead comes from FIRST(βa), not simply FOLLOW(B). Never shift the lookahead along with the dot.","Which lookahead is used for a completed CLR item?","Only the lookahead stored in that item.","lr"],[2,"LALR — merge matching LR(0) cores","Module 2 pp. 87–92","LALR compresses CLR states by merging states with identical item cores, combining lookahead sets.",["First construct CLR(1) collection. Strip lookaheads to compare LR(0) cores.","Merge equal cores and union lookaheads separately for each core item. Redirect transitions to merged state IDs.","Build ACTION and GOTO from merged states. Check conflicts, especially reduce/reduce conflicts introduced by merging.","Comparison: LR(0) has no reduction lookahead; SLR uses FOLLOW; CLR uses item lookahead; LALR merges CLR cores. LR(0)⊆SLR(1)⊆LALR(1)⊆LR(1) as grammar classes."],"I3: [C→d·,c/d]; I6: [C→d·,$]\nSame core C→d· → merge to [C→d·,c/d/$].\nDistinct cores must never merge.","LALR does not merge states just because they have the same number of items. Valid CLR can become conflicting in LALR.","What if two states have same core but different lookaheads?","Merge them and union lookaheads for each corresponding item.","lr"],[3,"SDD vs SDT and semantic attributes","Module 3 (2026) pp. 1–8, 13–20","A grammar says which strings are legal. Syntax-directed rules say what to do with the parse tree.",["SDD = CFG plus attributes and semantic rules; SDT = CFG plus executable semantic actions embedded in production RHS.","Synthesized attributes flow from children to parent. Inherited attributes obtain information from parent or siblings according to the definition.","S-attributed SDD: only synthesized attributes; evaluate bottom-up, naturally compatible with LR.","L-attributed: inherited attributes of RHS symbol Xj may depend on inherited attributes of A and attributes of symbols to its left (and restricted noncyclic self-dependencies); evaluate left-to-right depth-first.","Dependency graph has an edge from every used attribute instance to the instance computed from it. Topological sort is an evaluation order if acyclic."],"E→E1+T { E.val = E1.val + T.val }\nT→num  { T.val = num.lexval }\nD→T L  { L.in = T.type }\nL→id   { addType(id.entry,L.in) }","S-attributed is a subset of L-attributed. Inherited does not mean just parent; left siblings can supply values.","How does a type declaration pass float to all identifiers?","Use an inherited type attribute from T through the list L.","attribute"],[3,"Annotated trees, evaluation order & declarations","Module 3 (2026) pp. 9–16, 20–23","An annotated parse tree is a normal parse tree with computed values written at its nodes.",["Draw the grammar's parse tree (do not silently change precedence). Attach attributes to node instances.","Arithmetic evaluation: leaf num.lexval → synthesized T/F values → parent expressions.","Declaration type propagation: T.type computed from keyword; L.in inherited from T; list rule forwards type to each identifier.","Construct dependency edges from sources to destinations; evaluate in topological order. A cycle prevents ordinary one-pass evaluation."],"3*5+4: F.val=3,5,4; T.val=3*5=15; E.val=15+4=19.\nreal id1,id2,id3: T.type=real; L.in=real; propagate real to each symbol-table entry.","Attributes are attached to individual parse-tree nodes; two occurrences of E are not one shared E.val.","Which attribute gets calculated first in 3*5+4?","Leaf values, then 3*5, then the final addition.","attribute"],[3,"Infix → postfix/prefix, AST construction","Module 3 (2026) pp. 24–33","Postfix is a postorder traversal of the expression tree; prefix is preorder. The AST preserves meaning without grammar scaffolding.",["Make identifier/number leaf nodes; make operator nodes with operands as children (mkleaf/mknode).","For E→E1+T, E.node=mknode('+',E1.node,T.node).","Infix: left, operator, right; prefix: operator, left, right; postfix: left, right, operator.","SDT postfix action emits operator after both operands; synthesized string rules can build prefix without needing premature output.","Bottom-up evaluation attaches attribute values to stack entries and computes the LHS attribute on reduction."],"a+b*c\nAST: +(a,*(b,c))\nPrefix: + a * b c\nPostfix: a b c * +\nFor (a+b)*c: prefix * + a b c; postfix a b + c *","Do not emit + as soon as it is read if translating to postfix; wait until its right operand is processed.","Postfix of a*(b+c)?","a b c + *","tree"],[3,"Binary-to-decimal & counting reductions","Module 3 (2026) pp. 34–35","Semantic rules can evaluate binary digits or count what happened during parsing.",["For B→B1 bit, B.val=2×B1.val+bit.val; base B→bit gives bit.val.","This is the positional-number recurrence, evaluated bottom-up.","For reduction count, initialize a counter to zero and increment once per executed production reduction (excluding augmented acceptance unless the question specifies otherwise).","For annotated tree counts, sum child production counts plus one per production node under the stated convention."],"1011: 1 → 2×1+0=2 → 2×2+1=5 → 2×5+1=11\nGrammar B→B bit|bit; bit→0|1: four bit reductions + four B reductions = 8, excluding acceptance.","Binary conversion is not decimal digit concatenation. Clarify whether counting every production or a selected grammar rule.","Binary 1101 in decimal?","13: 1→3→6→13.","binary"],[4,"IR, syntax trees, DAG and three-address code","Module 4 (2026) pp. 1–12","Three-address code (TAC) splits a complicated expression into small instructions with at most one operator per typical arithmetic instruction.",["IR makes the front end independent from target machines. Common forms: AST, DAG, postfix notation and TAC.","AST has distinct expression occurrences; DAG can share identical subexpressions when operands refer to the same values.","TAC forms include x=y op z, x=op y, x=y, jumps, conditional jumps, param/call/return, indexed loads/stores, addresses and pointer operations.","Translate deepest/highest-precedence operation first, store in temporaries, then build outer expression."],"x = (a+b)*(a+b)\nAST repeats a+b twice; DAG shares it.\nTAC without reuse: t1=a+b; t2=a+b; t3=t1*t2; x=t3\nWith valid common-subexpression reuse: t1=a+b; t2=t1*t1; x=t2","Sharing is unsafe if an operand changes or expression has side effects. TAC does not mean exactly three operands in every instruction.","TAC for x=a+b*c?","t1=b*c; t2=a+t1; x=t2.","tree"],[4,"Quadruples, triples & indirect triples","Module 4 (2026) pp. 13–23","The same TAC can be stored three ways. Quadruples name temporary results; triples refer to instruction positions.",["Quadruple = (op,arg1,arg2,result). Use blank fields for unary operations as appropriate.","Triple = (op,arg1,arg2); result identified by instruction index. Assignment convention may be (=,destination,source) or another documented order; label columns.","Indirect triples keep triples fixed and add a separate pointer list; reorder execution by moving pointers rather than rewriting position references.","For function calls, retain param order, call target, argument count, and optional returned result. Follow the source's chosen convention."],"a=b+c*d\nTAC: t1=c*d; t2=b+t1; a=t2\nQUAD: (*,c,d,t1); (+,b,t1,t2); (=,t2,–,a)\nTRIPLE (result by index; assign dest,src): 0:(*,c,d); 1:(+,b,(0)); 2:(=,a,(1))\nINDIRECT: pointer list [0,1,2] → triples","Changing a triple row's index can invalidate other operands referencing it; indirect triples help avoid this.","What is the result of triple 0 used as?","Reference (0), not the literal integer zero.","ir"],[4,"Declarations, widths, types & offsets","Module 4 (2026) pp. 24–32","The compiler must reserve storage and record where each declared object lives.",["The 2026 lecture's 2D declaration example uses int=2, float=4, char=1 bytes. Other questions may specify different widths; actual layout depends on target and alignment.","Set offset=0 at scope start; for declaration x of type T, enter (x,T.type,offset) in symbol table; offset += T.width (align if required).","Array type array(n,T) has width n×T.width. Nested arrays recursively multiply dimensions.","A two-dimensional int[2][3] is array(2,array(3,int)) with six elements, width 12 in the lecture's 2-byte int model.","Maintain a separate symbol table/scope for procedure declarations as required."],"Faculty declaration (p. 30–32): int A[2][3], int width=2 → array(2,array(3,int)), elements=6, width=12.\nOriginal offset example (different explicit sizes, no alignment): int a width4 at offset0; real b width8 at offset4; int c[2][3] width24 at offset12; next offset36.","Do not assume real is always 8 or alignment absent. State assumptions when not supplied.","Width of int A[4][5] at 4 bytes/element?","80 bytes under the question's explicitly supplied 4-byte int model; 40 bytes under the lecture's 2-byte model.",""],[4,"Arrays, assignment translation & coercion","Module 4 (2026) pp. 33–39","An array address is base plus a byte offset; TAC explicitly computes that offset.",["1D address base+(i−L)w. 2D row-major address base+((i−L1)N2+(j−L2))w, where N2 is columns.","Column-major uses ((j−L2)N1+(i−L1))w. Do not mix conventions.","For x=A[i][j], calculate byte offset in temporaries, then load. For assignment to array element, emit indexed store.","Coercion: convert permitted incompatible operand types to a common type before performing operation. Explicit narrowing must obey language rules.","Temporaries may be reused after last use, but not while their previous values are still needed."],"A[3][5], base=1000, w=4, zero-based row-major.\nA[2][3]: t1=2*5=10; t2=t1+3=13; t3=t2*4=52\nAddress=1052. TAC: x=A[t3] (byte-offset convention).","N2 is number of columns, not rows, in row-major. Subtract lower bounds before multiplying.","For A[2][3] (2 rows, 3 cols), A[1][2] byte offset if w=4?","(1×3+2)×4=20.","array"],[4,"Boolean expressions and short-circuit translation","Module 4 (2026) pp. 40–50","Boolean expressions can be stored as 0/1, or used as branches that jump directly to the right code.",["Numerical representation computes Boolean values; short-circuit translation emits branches and avoids evaluating unnecessary operands.","For B1 && B2, true of B1 enters B2, false of B1 skips B2. For B1 || B2, false enters B2, true skips B2.","NOT swaps true and false destinations.","If/else emits conditional jump, then code, jump over else, else code, join. While emits test, body, back edge to test, exit.","For relational expression, emit if x relop y goto true; goto false. Materialize 0/1 only if a Boolean value is needed."],"if (a<b && c<d) x=1; else x=0;\n1 if a<b goto 3\n2 goto 7\n3 if c<d goto 5\n4 goto 7\n5 x=1\n6 goto 8\n7 x=0\n8 next","Do not evaluate c<d when a<b is false in short-circuit AND; side effects make eager evaluation different.","Which side of B1 OR B2 is evaluated if B1 is true?","B2 is skipped.","branch"],[4,"Backpatching — fill missing jump targets","Module 4 (2026) pp. 51–54; Module 4 (2025) pp. 41–45","Imagine writing a jump before knowing the line number. Keep a list of incomplete jumps, then fill targets when they become known.",["makelist(i) creates a list with instruction i; merge(p,q) joins lists; backpatch(p,i) fills target i into all incomplete jumps in p.","For relational B: truelist=makelist(nextinstr), falselist=makelist(nextinstr+1), emit conditional and unconditional unresolved jumps.","For B1 AND M B2, backpatch B1.truelist to M.instr; B.truelist=B2.truelist; B.falselist=merge(B1.falselist,B2.falselist).","For OR, backpatch B1.falselist to M.instr; B.truelist=merge(B1.truelist,B2.truelist); B.falselist=B2.falselist. NOT swaps lists.","Marker M records nextinstr; statement nextlist tracks jumps to following statements. For if/else patch true/false into respective branch starts and merge pending exits."],"a<b OR c<d\n1 if a<b goto _   T1={1}\n2 goto _          F1={2}\n3 if c<d goto _   T2={3}\n4 goto _          F2={4}\nPatch F1→3. Final T={1,3}; F={4}.\nIf true code starts 5 and false code 8: 1→5, 2→3, 3→5, 4→8.","AND patches true; OR patches false. Do not merge both lists indiscriminately.","For AND, which B1 list gets patched to B2's start?","B1.truelist.","backpatch"],[4,"Case statements, calls & full TAC patterns","Module 4 (2025) p. 45; Module 4 (2026) pp. 10–23, 46–54","Control flow can be represented by labels and branches even when source syntax is complex.",["Evaluate switch/case expression once; compare to each case, branch to labels, and implement default. Dense cases may use a jump table if suitable.","Preserve break/fallthrough semantics; the end label is shared by exiting cases.","Procedure calls: emit param instructions, call p,n, optionally assign returned value to temporary; return x transfers result.","For loops use initialization → test → body → increment → back edge; label exits explicitly.","Translate nested constructs inside out, using fresh temporaries/labels."],"switch(k) { case 1: x=10; break; default: x=0; }\nif k==1 goto L1\ngoto LD\nL1: x=10; goto LE\nLD: x=0\nLE: next\n\nz=f(a,b): param a; param b; t1=call f,2; z=t1","Do not add break automatically to a language with fallthrough if the source omits it.","Why evaluate switch expression only once?","Preserve evaluation/side effects and avoid duplicated work.",""],[5,"Optimization sources & loop transformations","Module 5 P1 (2026) pp. 1–16,40–46","Optimization makes equivalent code cheaper. Correctness comes before speed.",["Common subexpression elimination (CSE): reuse a previously computed expression if operands unchanged.","Copy propagation replaces copies; constant propagation substitutes known constants; folding evaluates constant expressions at compile time.","Dead code elimination removes results never used, provided no required side effects or traps.","Loop invariant code motion moves safe invariant computations to a preheader. Induction variables change by a fixed amount; strength reduction replaces expensive operations with cheaper equivalents.","Loop unrolling duplicates body, reducing branch overhead but needs remainder handling; fusion/jamming combines compatible loops; preserve dependences and semantics.","Local structure-preserving transformations include renaming temporaries and reordering independent statements."],"t1=a+b; t2=a+b; x=t2+0 → t1=a+b; x=t1\nfor i=0..n-1: t=k*4; A[i]=t+i\nIf safe: t=k*4 before loop, then A[i]=t+i.\nAddress induction: offset=0; each iteration offset+=4 instead of multiply.","A dead assignment is not automatically removable if its RHS performs I/O or may trap in an observable way. Floating-point algebraic rewrites need care.","Which optimization computes 3*4 at compile time?","Constant folding.","opt"],[5,"Basic blocks and control-flow graph (CFG)","Module 5 P1 pp. 28–38","A basic block is a straight-line sequence entered at its start and exited at its end. A CFG connects possible transfers of control.",["Leaders: first instruction, all branch targets, and any instruction immediately following a branch (if present).","Each block starts at a leader and continues up to just before the next leader.","Add an edge for each possible explicit jump; conditional branches also have fallthrough; unconditional jumps do not.","Entry and exit can be explicit artificial nodes. Unreachable blocks may be removed if truly unreachable.","A control-flow graph is not a context-free grammar even though both are abbreviated CFG."],"1 i=0\n2 if i>=10 goto 6\n3 sum=sum+i\n4 i=i+1\n5 goto 2\n6 return sum\nLeaders 1,2,3,6; blocks B1=[1], B2=[2], B3=[3–5], B4=[6].\nEdges B1→B2; B2→B3,B4; B3→B2.","The branch instruction belongs to its own preceding block. A following instruction is a leader even if unreachable.","Which block has a back edge?","B3→B2.","cfg"],[5,"Dataflow: reaching definitions, available expressions, liveness","Module 5 P1 pp. 17–27","Dataflow analysis asks what information is true at each program point across possible control paths.",["Reaching definitions: forward may analysis; IN[B]=union OUT[pred]; OUT=GEN∪(IN−KILL). A definition reaches if no intervening redefinition along some path.","Available expressions: forward must analysis; IN[B]=intersection OUT[pred]; OUT=GEN∪(IN−KILL). Expression computed on all paths with no subsequent operand redefinition.","Live variables: backward may analysis; OUT[B]=union IN[succ]; IN=USE∪(OUT−DEF). Live means a future use before redefinition on some path.","Initialize boundary appropriately: RD entry empty (or external definitions), liveness exit per live-out convention, AE entry empty and other initial sets universal. Iterate until fixed point.","For sequence S1;S2, reaching-def GEN=GEN2∪(GEN1−KILL2); KILL convention depends on block definitions/universe. For branches, merge alternatives with union for may."],"B1: d1:x=1 → B2 or B3\nB2: d2:x=2 → B4\nB3: no x assignment → B4\nRD at B4={d1,d2}. Available x+1 only if computed on BOTH paths after final x definition.\nIf B4: y=x+1, x is live on entry B4.","May = union; must = intersection. Liveness flows backward, not forward.","Why is x live before y=x+1?","Its current value will be read before being overwritten.","dataflow"],[5,"DAG of a basic block & regeneration","Module 5 P1 pp. 56–63; M5-P3 slides 10–29","A DAG represents values and their computations. Identical live expressions share nodes, so redundant work becomes visible.",["For x=y op z, locate current nodes of y,z (create initial leaves if needed). Reuse an existing op with identical ordered children if legal, otherwise create one.","Remove label x from its previous node, attach x to new node. For x=y, attach x to node(y). For unary operation reuse matching one-child node.","Nodes with output/live variable labels must be preserved; remove unused pure computation only when safe.","Generate code in an order satisfying child-before-parent dependencies. Rearrangement/heuristic ordering may reduce register spills; the lecture's labeling algorithm is a register-need guide.","For binary tree under simple register model, equal child labels k give k+1; unequal gives max. Leaf conventions depend on machine model (some notes use right-leaf zero); follow the stated convention."],"t1=a+b; t2=a+b; c=t1*t2; a=7; t3=a+b\nDAG shares first + node for t1,t2. c uses it twice.\nAfter a=7, t3 uses NEW a node, so cannot reuse original a+b.\nFor t1=a+b; t2=c+d; t3=e−t2; t4=t1−t3, evaluate children before parents; choose order to limit simultaneous live values.","Variables label changing values; the leaf a at entry is not the same value as a after assignment.","Why cannot the third a+b reuse the first after a=7?","Because the operand value of a changed.","dag"],[5,"Peephole optimization","Module 5 P1 pp. 47–55","Look at a small window of target instructions and replace inefficient patterns with equivalent ones.",["Redundant load/store removal: eliminate unnecessary transfers only if intervening operations do not change the value or make the transfer observable.","Remove unreachable code; simplify jumps to jumps; eliminate jump to immediately next instruction when appropriate.","Algebraic simplification (x+0), strength reduction (multiply by power of two → shift under valid semantics), use of machine idioms.","Repeated application may uncover new opportunities; preserve flags, exceptions, memory/alias behavior and control-flow semantics."],"MOV R1,R2; MOV R2,R1 → second redundant if registers unchanged and semantics permit\ngoto L1; L1: goto L2 → goto L2 (redirect)\nx=x+0 → remove if no side effect and identity valid\nx=8*y → shift left 3 if integer semantics permit.","Peephole is local to a small instruction window; global dataflow reasons across blocks.","What technique replaces a jump to a jump with a direct jump?","Flow-of-control peephole optimization.","opt"],[5,"Dominators, natural loops & reducibility","M5-P3 slides 2–9; Module 5 (2025) pp. 64–72","A dominator is a checkpoint every path to a node must pass through. A natural loop is a single-entry region with a back edge.",["d dominates n if every entry→n path passes through d; each node dominates itself.","Compute Dom(entry)={entry}; other nodes initially all reachable nodes; Dom(n)={n}∪intersection Dom(pred). Iterate.","Back edge n→d when d dominates n. Natural loop = d plus nodes reaching n without passing through d; collect by reverse traversal of predecessors.","Header dominates loop nodes; preheader is a unique outside predecessor block inserted for safe loop-invariant work. Innermost loop contains no other loop.","Reducible graph can separate acyclic forward edges and back edges to dominators; irreducible cyclic regions may have multiple entries. Not every backward-looking arrow is a back edge."],"CFG 1→2→3→4, 4→2, 2→5.\nDom(1)={1}; Dom(2)={1,2}; Dom(3)={1,2,3}; Dom(4)={1,2,3,4}; Dom(5)={1,2,5}.\n4→2 back edge; natural loop {2,3,4}; header=2; preheader can be 1 if appropriate.","The header dominates the tail, not necessarily vice versa. Do not call every cycle a natural loop.","What is natural loop of 4→2 here?","{2,3,4}.","dom"],[5,"Native code generation in VMs, JIT & security","Module 5 (2025) pp. 73–107 (supplementary older full deck)","A VM can execute portable intermediate code and compile hot code to native instructions at runtime.",["Distinguish language VM (bytecode/runtime abstraction) from system VM (virtual hardware/hypervisor); lecture covers both.","JIT compilation profiles execution, selects hot methods, may inline calls and apply local/global/control-flow optimizations, then emits native code. AOT compiles before execution; interpreter executes without native compilation of the whole program.","VM bytecode verification checks instruction structure, type/stack consistency, valid branch targets and access constraints before execution.","Dynamic checks include bounds checks, null checks, access enforcement and runtime resource controls. Sandbox/verification reduce risk from untrusted code.","Benefits portability and dynamic specialization; costs startup/warm-up, compilation overhead, and runtime complexity. Historical JVM security-manager slide is legacy context, not a universal current JVM feature."],"source → bytecode → verifier → interpreter/profile → JIT hot code → native machine code\nExample: reject a bytecode instruction that adds incompatible stack operand types; runtime array bounds check rejects invalid index.","Verification is not the same as optimization and cannot eliminate all runtime checks.","Static vs dynamic verification?","Static occurs before execution; dynamic checks occur during execution.",""],[6,"Code generator responsibilities & target machine","Module 6 consolidated (2026) slides 2–28; M6-P1 slides 2–27","The code generator chooses real machine instructions for IR while preserving semantics.",["Three tasks: instruction selection, register allocation/assignment, instruction ordering. Inputs: IR plus symbol-table/type/storage information.","Target output may be absolute machine code, relocatable machine code or assembly. Target ISA and addressing modes affect instruction selection.","Memory management maps names to addresses and storage classes; evaluation order affects register pressure.","Target instructions in lectures use forms such as MOV/LD/ST, arithmetic, jumps, conditional jumps and indexed/indirect addressing.","Correctness first, then code quality (size/speed) and compilation cost. Distinguish register allocation (which values) from assignment (which physical register)."],"TAC x=y+z\nPossible target (source,dest notation): MOV y,R0; ADD z,R0; MOV R0,x\nIf y already resides in R0, first move may be unnecessary.","Always state instruction operand convention. Different ISAs use different destination orders.","What does register assignment decide?","Which specific physical register holds an allocated value.",""],[6,"Instruction cost numericals","Module 6 consolidated slides 29–34; 2025 Code Generation pp. 20–25","Instruction cost reflects opcode plus addressing-mode costs in the lecture's abstract machine, not a universal real CPU latency.",["Lecture cost convention: 1 for instruction plus 1 for each operand needing an extra memory word (e.g. absolute address or immediate constant); register operands add zero. Use supplied mode table if different.","Count each instruction separately; compare equivalent sequences. Register reuse and addressing modes can avoid memory moves.","Do not equate static instruction cost with cycle count on a pipelined processor."],"Illustrative lecture-style cost:\nMOV a,R0 = 1+1+0=2\nADD b,R0 = 1+1+0=2\nMOV R0,x = 1+0+1=2\nTotal 6.\nMOV R0,R1 costs 1 under this convention.","An immediate constant may require an extra word in this model; don't assume it is free just because not a memory load.","Cost of MOV #5,R1 if immediate costs 1?","2.","cost"],[6,"Next-use & liveness, register/address descriptors","M6-P2 slides 2–23; Register Allocation & Assignment slides 6–9","The compiler scans backward to learn which values will be needed again, then uses that knowledge to avoid needless loads and stores.",["Next use = next statement that reads a value before redefinition; live = needed along a future path. Initialize variables live-out according to question; temporaries typically dead unless specified.","Scan basic block backwards. For x=y op z, record current after-statement info for x,y,z; kill x (dead/no next use), then mark y,z live with next use at current instruction. If x also appears on RHS, uses must be processed after kill.","Register descriptor tracks names whose current values reside in each register; address descriptor tracks locations holding each name's current value (memory/registers).","When writing a register, invalidate overwritten associations; when assigning x, remove stale x locations and record new ones. Store live-out dirty variables at block exit.","getReg prefers suitable existing operand register if safe, then free register, otherwise choose spill with low cost/next use."],"1 t=a+b\n2 u=t+c\n3 x=u+t\nAssume only x live on exit. Before 3: u,t live, next use 3. Before 2: t,c live, next use 2 for t,c (t also used at 3). Before 1: a,b,c live (assuming these uses), t's old value dead.\nAt forward generation, keep t in register through statement 3 if possible.","Next-use information is per value at a point, not a single count for the whole variable lifetime.","If x=x+1, should x be live before the instruction?","Yes; kill the old definition as a destination then mark RHS use.","next"],[6,"Register allocation: usage counts, coloring, linear scan","M6-P2 slides 26–36; consolidated slides 42–47; Register Allocation & Assignment deck","There are fewer hardware registers than program variables. Allocation decides what to keep in fast storage.",["Global allocation may prioritize frequently used loop variables. Usage-count heuristic weights savings from uses and live exits; use formula provided by question (lecture commonly uses use(x,B)+2 live(x,B)).","Interference graph: node per live range; edge if two values must coexist. Color graph with K colors/registers; adjacent nodes cannot share a color.","Simplify by removing degree<K nodes; if none, choose potential spill; pop/reinsert, color with available register; spill if no color fits. Greedy degree ordering is a simpler lecture heuristic, not guaranteed minimum.","Linear scan: sort live intervals by start; expire ended active intervals; allocate free register; if none, spill interval using policy (e.g. farthest end).","Local allocation may need reconciliation moves at control-flow joins. Register spills save/reload values in memory. Evaluation order affects pressure."],"Interference edges AB, AC, BC, CD.\nA=R1, B=R2, C=R3, D=R1 valid. Triangle ABC forces at least 3 registers.\nIf only 2 registers, at least one triangle live range must be spilled or transformed.","Interference is overlap requiring distinct storage, not just names appearing in the same program. Greedy coloring result isn't necessarily chromatic minimum.","Can A and D share R1 in this graph?","Yes, no edge AD.","color"],[6,"Runtime storage, activation trees & records","Module 6 consolidated slides 48–69; M6-P2 slides 37–42","A function call needs a place to keep its parameters, local variables and return information. That place is an activation record.",["Runtime memory regions: code, static/global data, heap (dynamic objects), stack (nested activations). Actual layout depends on platform.","Static allocation fixes storage before runtime; stack allocation fits LIFO call/return; heap supports arbitrary lifetimes and dynamic objects.","An activation is one execution of a procedure. Activation tree root is main; children represent calls in order. Stack at a moment is the root-to-current-node path.","Activation record may contain arguments, return value/address, control/dynamic link (caller), access/static link (lexical scope), saved registers, local variables and temporaries.","Recursion creates distinct records per activation. Parameter passing may be by value, reference or other language-defined mechanisms; access link not necessarily same as control link."],"main calls f(2); f(2) calls f(1); f(1) calls f(0).\nAt deepest point stack: main → f(2) → f(1) → f(0).\nOn return pop f(0), then f(1), then f(2).","Stack frame is not a single global frame shared by all invocations. Activation tree contains completed calls too; active stack is only one path.","Which region holds dynamically allocated objects with arbitrary lifetimes?","Heap.","stack"],[7,"Parallelization, loop normalization & induction substitution","Module 7 consolidated slides 1–22; M7-P1 slides 2–12; 2025 Module 7 pp. 2–20","Parallelization runs independent work simultaneously. Compiler must prove transformations preserve program behavior.",["Distinguish concurrency (overlapping progress) from parallel execution (simultaneous work). Levels include instruction, data and task parallelism.","Automatic parallelization: analyze dependence, transform loops, schedule/map work; not every loop is profitable or safe.","Normalize lower bound and stride, often i=1..N with unit increment. Replace original variable by L+(i−1)s; N=floor((U−L)/s)+1 for inclusive increasing loop with s>0.","Induction variable substitution rewrites derived variables as linear functions of loop index. Forward substitution and expression folding expose analyzable array subscripts.","Example independent A[i]=B[i]+C[i] can vectorize assuming no harmful alias; recurrence A[i+1]=A[i]+B[i] has loop-carried true dependence."],"for i=3 to 11 step 2: A[i]=i*4\nN=floor((11−3)/2)+1=5\nfor k=1..5: A[3+2*(k−1)] = 4*(3+2*(k−1))\nOriginal values 3,5,7,9,11.","Normalization must preserve original iteration values and count. Don't treat loops with unknown bounds/aliases as automatically safe.","Normalize i=2..10 step 2 to k=1..5.","i=2+2(k−1)=2k.","normalize"],[7,"RAW, WAR, WAW, distance/direction & DDG","Module 7 consolidated slides 23–28,42–47; M7-P1 slides 13–18","Dependence tells you which operations cannot be freely reordered. Read and write order is the key.",["RAW (flow/true): earlier write, later read. WAR (anti): earlier read, later write. WAW (output): two writes to same location. RAR is not a data dependence.","Determine whether two array accesses refer to same memory location by equating subscripts and checking iteration bounds/order.","Distance vector = sink iteration − source iteration; direction vector compares source and sink indices (<,=,>) for each loop dimension. State this convention since notations can differ.","Loop-carried dependence crosses iteration boundaries. A dependence with first non-equal direction < is forward under lexicographic execution. A same-iteration dependence has all =.","DDG nodes are statements/operations; directed edges record required order, with type/distance/direction. Independent iterations can vectorize; loop-carried cycles require transformation or sequential execution.","WAR/WAW can sometimes be removed by renaming/privatization; RAW is true data flow. For dependence cycles, strongly connected components constrain vectorization."],"S1: A[i]=B[i]+1\nS2: C[i]=A[i-1]*2\nS1 at iteration i produces A[i]; S2 at iteration i+1 reads it.\nRAW distance +1, direction <, loop-carried.\nFor S1: x=1; S2: y=x+2 → RAW.\nFor S1: y=x+2; S2: x=1 → WAR.\nFor S1: x=1; S2: x=2 → WAW.","Direction compares source to sink, so distance +1 corresponds to < with that convention. A loop-carried dependence does not mean every possible vectorization transform is impossible.","What dependence occurs when read precedes write?","WAR (anti-dependence).","depend"],[7,"Cache locality, SIMD & DSLs","2025 Module 7 pp. 40–75, 123–132 (supplementary full deck)","Hardware likes predictable nearby memory and independent operations. Domain knowledge lets compilers exploit them.",["Temporal locality: reuse same location soon; spatial locality: access nearby addresses. Row-major arrays favor contiguous inner-column access.","Loop interchange, tiling/blocking, fusion, layout and prefetching can improve locality only when dependences permit.","SIMD runs same operation on multiple lanes. Auto-vectorizer checks dependence, aliasing, alignment and profitability; handles scalar remainder/masks.","DSL targets a narrow domain (SQL, tensor expressions). Internal DSL uses host syntax/libraries; external DSL has own syntax/parser. Domain-specific constraints and optimizations can improve correctness and performance.","Compilation strategies: interpreter, source-to-source transpiler or native/IR backend. Language design (types, GC, dynamic features) and architecture (SIMD, multicore) shape compiler decisions."],"Row-major A[i][j]: iterate j innermost for contiguous elements.\nTiling matrix multiply: process blocks of rows/columns so reused data fits cache.\nSIMD: [1,2,3,4]+[5,6,7,8]=[6,8,10,12].","Vectorization isn't the same as multithreading: SIMD lanes vs multiple threads. Loop interchange is illegal if dependence order changes.","Which locality describes accessing A[i], A[i+1], A[i+2]?","Spatial locality.",""],[7,"Instruction scheduling & dependence DAG","Module 7 consolidated slides 29–49; M7-P1 slides 19–31","Reorder instructions so independent operations fill stalls, but never use a value before it exists.",["Instruction scheduling maps each operation to a start cycle while respecting dependencies, latencies and hardware resource constraints.","Dependence graph nodes = operations, edges = precedence with latency constraints. RAW, WAR, WAW and memory/control dependencies may matter.","For edge u→v with latency L(u,v), start(v)≥start(u)+L. Independent operations may overlap on different functional units or issue slots.","Critical path is maximum weighted dependence path and gives a lower bound on schedule length. Resource constraints may increase it.","Compiler-inserted NOPs may be needed on machines without hardware interlocks; reordering can fill gaps if legal."],"A: load x (latency 2); B: add using A (1); C: independent multiply (2).\nEdges A→B. With two compatible issue slots: cycle 0 A,C; cycle 2 B. Completion cycle boundary 3.\nWith one issue slot, schedule A at 0, C at 1, B at 2 if units permit.","A node is ready only after predecessor latency requirements, not simply after predecessor issue. State cycle origin and latency assumptions.","Can B start at cycle 1 if A started at 0 and has latency 2?","No. Earliest cycle 2.","schedule"],[7,"List scheduling — priorities and ready list","Module 7 consolidated slides 50–74; M7-P1 slides 32–37","List scheduling is a greedy algorithm: among operations ready now, pick those with highest priority that fit available resources.",["Build DAG, optionally rename removable false dependencies; compute priority (e.g. longest path to exit).","Maintain unscheduled nodes, ready list and resource availability. At each cycle add operations whose predecessors have completed.","Select ready highest priority operation that fits resources, issue it, update ready times. If no feasible operation, advance cycle.","Repeat until all scheduled; report cycle table, length and resource use. Tie-break deterministically, e.g. lowest node ID.","Critical-path priority improves heuristic decisions but does not guarantee an optimal schedule."],"A(2)→C(1)→E(1); B(1)→D(2)→E(1)\nHeights A=4,B=4,C=2,D=3,E=1. Two fully pipelined slots: cycle0 A,B; cycle1 D; cycle2 C; cycle3 E; done at 4.\nWith one slot, use priority and resource checks to construct schedule.","A schedule with legal dependence order may still be invalid if too many instructions use the same functional unit.","Why choose critical-path operations earlier?","Delaying them can delay the earliest possible finish of the whole schedule.","schedule"],[7,"Software pipelining & modulo reservation table","Module 7 consolidated slides 75–89; M7-P2 slides 1–12","Instead of finishing all operations of iteration 1 before iteration 2, overlap different stages from different iterations.",["Prologue fills pipeline; kernel executes steady-state overlapped schedule; epilogue drains it.","Initiation interval II = cycles between starts of successive iterations. Lower bound max(ResMII,RecMII).","Resource bound: ceil(uses per iteration / units available per cycle), max across resources. Recurrence bound: ceil(total latency / total dependence distance) for each positive-distance cycle.","For dependence u(i)→v(i+d), schedule must satisfy t(v)−t(u)≥latency−d×II. Check all dependence/resource constraints.","Modulo reservation table maps resource usage to cycle t mod II; operations of different iterations can conflict in the same modulo slot. Raise II or change placement if impossible.","Loop unrolling duplicates iterations; software pipelining overlaps stages. They can be combined but are different transformations."],"Three stages Load, Compute, Store each 1 cycle on separate units.\ncycle0 L0\ncycle1 L1 C0\ncycle2 L2 C1 S0\ncycle3 C2 S1\ncycle4 S2\nFor 3 iterations length=5 cycles vs 9 sequential, II=1 under assumed independent stages/resources.\nIf recurrence latency 5, distance 2: RecMII≥ceil(5/2)=3.","II is not total loop latency. The lower bound isn't necessarily feasible. Include prologue and epilogue.","If a loop needs 5 uses of one resource and 2 units per cycle, resource bound?","ceil(5/2)=3.","pipe"],[7,"SSA, φ placement & renaming","Module 7 consolidated slides 90–107; 2025 Module 7 pp. 133–146","Static Single Assignment gives every definition a unique name, so each use has a single defining instruction.",["Rename each assignment to a new version; at a join where distinct reaching values meet, insert φ with one operand per predecessor.","A φ selects according to the incoming CFG edge; it is not an arithmetic sum or runtime if statement in ordinary IR semantics.","Dominance frontier DF(d) contains nodes where d dominates a predecessor but does not strictly dominate the node. Use iterated DF of definition blocks for φ insertion (pruned SSA may use liveness).","Rename via dominator-tree traversal using stacks per original variable; rename uses to current top, push new definitions, fill successor φ arguments for each edge, pop on return.","Loops need φ at header to merge initial and back-edge values. Each SSA definition dominates its ordinary uses (φ operands treated on incoming edges)."],"if c: x=1 else x=2; y=x+3\nB1: x1=1\nB2: x2=2\nB3: x3=φ(B1:x1,B2:x2); y1=x3+3\nLoop header: i2=φ(entry:i1,back:i3); body i3=i2+1.","A φ must have predecessor-specific operands, not just arbitrary versions. SSA is static property, not that a loop instruction runs only once.","What is x3 if control comes from B2?","x2, whose value is 2.","ssa"],[7,"SSA optimization: DCE, CCP, value numbering & liveness","Module 7 consolidated slides 108–147","SSA makes def-use relationships explicit, simplifying optimization and analysis.",["Dead-code elimination: mark observable roots (returns, effects, required control), propagate to defining instructions through uses; remove unmarked safe instructions.","Conditional constant propagation (CCP/SCCP): lattice unknown/undefined → constant → overdefined; track executable edges, evaluate φ only from executable predecessors, iterate via worklist.","If branch condition becomes constant, only feasible edge contributes. Conflicting constants at executable φ inputs become overdefined.","Global value numbering: assign equivalent pure expressions equal numbers based on operand value numbers and operation. Traverse in dominator order; replace only with available dominating definition. Back edges/φ may require iteration.","SSA liveness: walk backwards from each use until definition, treating φ uses on predecessor edges. Dominance and def-use relationships must stay valid after transformations."],"x1=2; y1=x1+3; z1=x1+3; return z1\nCCP: y1=z1=5. Value numbering: same + of same operand values; reuse y1 if dominating. DCE: remove unused pure instructions after propagation, e.g. return 5.\nBranch c=true; x3=φ(then:4,else:9) → 4 if else edge not executable.","Do not merge expressions if operand versions differ, memory side effects invalidate them, or the replacement doesn't dominate the use.","If two executable φ inputs have constants 4 and 9, what lattice value?","Overdefined (not a single known constant).","ssa"]]};